<details class="debrief">
  <summary>What this exercise is showing</summary>

  <p>WCAG 2.2 added <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/target-size-minimum.html">2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum)</a>, which asks for 24 by 24 CSS pixels, or enough spacing around a smaller target that a 24 pixel circle centred on it would not overlap its neighbour. The AAA version, 2.5.5, asks for 44 by 44 &mdash; which is what Apple and Google have both recommended for years, and what most people's thumbs actually need.</p>

  <p>Size is only half of it. The other half is <strong>what sits next to what</strong>. Putting <em>Cancel order</em> beside <em>Pay now</em>, the same size and a few pixels apart, means that every slip is expensive. Good design puts distance, or a different weight, or a confirmation step between a mistake and its consequence.</p>

  <p>This is not only about permanent disability. It is a bus, a cold morning, a cracked screen, a cast on your wrist, a baby on your hip, the last 3% of your battery. Design for the worst moment, not the best one.</p>

  <h3>Questions for the group</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>What did the measurements say? Was anything under 24 pixels? Was anything under 44?</li>
    <li>Where in our product does a destructive or expensive action sit next to a routine one?</li>
    <li>What is our smallest tap target &mdash; a close button, a checkbox, a pagination link, an icon in a table row?</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What good looks like</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>44 by 44 as the working minimum for anything you tap, with 24 as the absolute floor.</li>
    <li>Space between targets, not just size. The gap is part of the target.</li>
    <li>The dangerous action looks different, sits apart, and ideally asks again.</li>
    <li>Cookie banners do not cover the thing people came to do, and they can be dismissed with one obvious, full-size control.</li>
  </ul>
</details>
